{
  "statement": "Accelerationists claim that the 2011 global wave of horizontal protest failed because leaderless movements cannot build lasting institutions, meaning technological speed and central control are our only routes to progress. That argument contains a grain of truth: decentralized camps in Zuccotti Park and Tahrir Square did not draft legislation or run banks. The accelerationist mistake is treating that horizontal collapse as proof that corporate monopolies are a valid substitute for democratic power. In reality, the industrial concentration of foundational compute infrastructure in 2026 mirrors the exact financialized hierarchies that people took to the streets to dismantle in 2011.\n\nWhen three firms control eighty percent of specialized AI chips, they are not building liberation tools; they are locking in private fiefdoms. The 2011 struggles cycle emerged precisely because citizens realized that deregulation and corporate scale concentrate wealth while socializing ruin. Today's tech boosters repackage this exact enclosure model as radical innovation. They want us to believe that speed and proprietary scale are moral imperatives that override local labor conditions and energy grid limits. That narrative functions as a strategic blindfold designed to protect elite capital from public scrutiny.\n\nFixing these imbalances requires direct anti-monopoly enforcement through federal antitrust division lawsuits and strict compute caps, not more techno-optimist rhetoric. The enforcement mechanism already exists in the Sherman Antitrust Act and Federal Trade Commission statutory authority. The political coalition to back this enforcement spans municipal governments facing soaring data center electricity bills and labor unions representing automated warehouse workers. History shows that when corporate monopolies control critical infrastructure, as railroads did in the late nineteenth century, public pushback is inevitable. The question is not whether the current AI centralization will face a reckoning, but how many local communities will bear the cost of grid strain and labor displacement while waiting for it.",
